2005</year><volume>54</volume><fpage>15</fpage><lpage>24</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16050324</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.3200/JACH.54.1.15-24</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ratliff-Crain</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Kane</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Predictors for altering caffeine consumption during stress</article-title><source>Addictive Behavior</source><year>1995</year><volume>20</volume><fpage>509</fpage><lpage>516</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/0306-4603(95)00012-2</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="book"><source>The relationship between caffeine consumption and study habits</source><publisher-name>Missouri Western Clearinghouse</publisher-name><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://clearinghouse.missouriwestern.edu/manuscripts/205.asp"/></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Anderson</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Horne</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A high sugar content, low caffeine drink does not alleviate sleepiness by may worsen it</article-title><source>Human Psychopharmacology: Clinical and Experimental</source><year>2006</year><volume>21</volume><fpage>299</fpage><lpage>303</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16856218</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/hup.769</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kohler</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Pavy</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Van Den Heuvel</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The effects of chewing versus caffeine on alertness, cognitive performance and cardiac autonomic activity during sleep deprivation</article-title><source>Journal of Sleep Research</source><year>2006</year><volume>15</volume><fpage>358</fpage><lpage>368</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17118092</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/j.1365-2869.2006.00547.x</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bridler</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Remick</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Duffy</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Is caffeine excess part of your differential diagnosis?</article-title><source>The Nurse Practitioner</source><year>2004</year><volume>29</volume><fpage>39</fpage><lpage>44</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15064573</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1097/00006205-200404000-00007</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B25"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pollak</surname><given-names>CP</given-names></name><name><surname>Bright</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Caffeine consumption and weekly sleep patterns in US seventh-, eighth-, and ninth-graders</article-title><source>Pediatrics</source><year>2007</year><volume>111</volume><fpage>42</fpage><lpage>46</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12509552</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1542/peds.111.1.42</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B26"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Strouse</surname><given-names>JS</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>College bars as social settings for heterosexual contacts</article-title><source>Journal of Sexual Research</source><year>1987</year><volume>23</volume><fpage>374</fpage><lpage>382</lpage></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>